[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: StrictDoc tooling plan backlog

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-PLAN-SCOPED-BACKLOG
TITLE: Each plan carries its own backlog document, and no new node type
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
An item needs to be tracked while living outside its plan's backlog document,
at which point an explicit register node and a Backlogged_In role become the
cheaper answer.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Every plan directory may hold a 99-backlog document. Anything noticed but not
groomed goes there as an ordinary node at DEPTH sketch. Grooming means moving
the node into a numbered document in the same plan and raising its DEPTH; the
UID never changes, so nothing that cites it breaks.

The plan's ungroomed set is therefore the query DEPTH == sketch scoped to that
plan's directory. Debt remaining when a plan closes is whatever is still at
sketch, and what to do with it is not decided here.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A dedicated BACKLOG node type was considered and rejected. The existing types
already say what kind of work an item is -- a MECHANISM to build, a DECISION to
make, a SPIKE to run -- and a separate type would throw that away and need
mapping back on grooming. Containment by document needs no grammar change at
all, and DEPTH sketch already means exactly ungroomed.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Deliberately not solved here: a repo-level backlog above plans. That decision
waits for a plan to actually close with debt on it.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LOG-DONT-SURFACE
TITLE: Incidental findings go to the backlog, not to session output
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The backlog grows faster than it is groomed, which would mean the filter is
letting too much through and the threshold needs restating.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
A model working in this repository may add backlog nodes on its own initiative,
without asking. It should.

Session output raises a finding only when it matters SOON -- it changes the
decision in front of the operator, or it blocks the work in hand. Everything
else, the whole class of "one more thing you should know", is written to the
plan's backlog document and not spoken. A finding that is logged is not lost, so
mentioning it costs attention while logging it costs nothing.

This applies to planning and implementation sessions alike.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator's judgment budget is the scarce resource, not disk or tokens.
Surfacing everything spends that budget on things that are not urgent, and the
predictable result is that genuinely urgent findings get skimmed along with the
rest.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-TREEFMT-SDOC
TITLE: Register strictdoc format as the treefmt formatter for sdoc and sgra
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
treefmt currently formats nothing with an sdoc or sgra extension: there is no
catch-all and unmatched files only warn. The canonical formatter for both is
strictdoc format, which is idempotent over this corpus and currently a no-op
because the files were written in canonical form by hand.

Wire it in treefmt.nix so formatting is enforced rather than incidental.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Two things depend on canonical form. Fingerprints are taken over field text, so
an unformatted file hashes differently from the same content formatted. And the
commit gate runs treefmt with fail-on-change, so anything treefmt does not know
about cannot be enforced there.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Watch for two things when doing it. strictdoc format takes a project directory,
not a file list, which is an awkward fit for treefmt's per-file invocation
model. And it mints a document MID on first run for any document lacking one,
which is a content change treefmt would report as a reformat.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-CI-CHECK
TITLE: Gate the design graph in nix flake check
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Add a flake check that exports the graph to JSON and fails on non-zero exit, so
a broken relation or an invalid enum value cannot merge.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Validation currently runs only when someone remembers to run it. The export is
a few seconds on a corpus this size.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The check must use a clean output directory. Incremental generation produces a
false green: the same broken input exits 1, then 0 on re-run against a warm
output directory.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT
TITLE: Every PARENT_FP entry should have a matching relation
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A node recording a fingerprint for a parent asserts a dependency on that
parent's contract, so it should also carry an edge to it. Nothing enforces
that today, and several nodes in this graph already have a fingerprint with no
corresponding relation.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Without the lint the two can disagree silently, and the edge is what the
traversal follows. A fingerprint the traversal cannot reach is invisible to both
the drift check and the render cache.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SGRA-BODY-COVERAGE
TITLE: Extend the sdoc tree-sitter grammar to cover document-body content
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured while implementing SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE:
checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix parses strictdoc-project/strictdoc's own
docs/ (15 files, pinned rev 215cda2e) and gets 5/15 clean under the patched
grammar, unchanged from baseline. The 10 failures are unrelated to
DEC-GRAMMAR-PATCH-NOT-FORK's patch (root-rule choice + REVERSE_ROLE) -- they
are document-body / FREETEXT coverage gaps in the upstream grammar itself,
present before and after this patch.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Out of scope for the current patch, which targets exactly the two bugs this
repository's own corpus hit. Worth a dedicated SPIKE to characterize the
FREETEXT gap before deciding whether it is a small follow-up patch or a
reason to look upstream harder.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH
TITLE: Work accumulates on one long-lived branch, restacked into pull requests later
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first restack completes and the branch merges, at which point the next unit
of work chooses its own shape.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
No pull request is opened during development and none is offered. Work
accumulates on one branch until the operator calls a milestone, then it is
restacked into a series of reviewable pull requests.

Two reviews, with different subjects. The stack is reviewed as a whole for
usability, design and flow. The pull requests are reviewed for implementation
choices. Operator acceptance is turned at the second of these, never before.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Commit boundaries are what make the restack legible, which is why worker
sessions commit per unit of work rather than once at the end. And there is no
signature to turn yet: the fingerprints are placeholders until the suspect-link
check exists, so acceptance has nothing to attach to before then.
<<<

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-RESTACK-TO-PRS
TITLE: Restack the long-lived branch into reviewable pull requests
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Cut the accumulated branch into a series of pull requests ordered so each is
independently reviewable, and land them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Slotted as future work so the strategy is tracked rather than remembered. Not
groomed: the cut points depend on what has accumulated by the milestone.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD
TITLE: Reject an AUTHORED_BY flip on a node already merged to main
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
A static check that fails when AUTHORED_BY changes from llm to human on a node
whose current value in main is llm. Operator override available, since the
operator is the only party entitled to make that flip.

Could run as a commit hook rather than only in the merge gate, if being
pedantic is worth the friction.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
AUTHORED_BY is the operator's key and nothing currently stops an agent turning
it. A skill rule forbids it, which is guidance rather than enforcement.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SEMBLE-TEST-RECIPE
TITLE: Make a customized semble testable without a devenv shell
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Expose a documented way to build and exercise a grammar-customized semble.
Today it takes a hand-written impure expression against the flake, and the
invocation must scrub PYTHONPATH and redirect the cache.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Measured while verifying SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE. An installed semble exports
PYTHONPATH, and that shadows any other build: pointing at a freshly built
binary silently runs the installed one with the installed mappings. The failure
presents as the new grammar not working, which is a false defect report waiting
to happen. It already caused one.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CHUNK-LEADING-WHITESPACE
TITLE: Chunks can begin on inter-node whitespace instead of the node header
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on an 8762-byte document: of 17 AST chunks, roughly five begin on a
blank line between nodes rather than on the node header or field start that
follows. Semble's sibling-merging walk treats whitespace tokens as ordinary
children and folds them into the front of the next group.

Cosmetic. The chunk still covers the right content and search results are
correct; the leading blank lines waste a little of the embedding window and
make a raw chunk read slightly worse.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Logged rather than fixed. Any fix lives upstream in semble's chunker or in a
grammar change that stops emitting whitespace as a sibling, and neither is
worth it for the observed cost. A good candidate to hand to an execution engine
later rather than to spend design attention on now.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE
TITLE: Which fields are contract-bearing is configuration, not a constant
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The Nix option surface for custom fields and workflow mechanics exists and the
operator has reviewed the field selection through it.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The set of fields a fingerprint covers is a per-project option, not a hardcoded
list in a script. A prototype may hardcode any reasonable selection to exercise
the logic, but that selection is not settled by having been prototyped.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The selection is the whole mechanism: too broad and every wording change
re-suspects everything until sign-off becomes a rubber stamp, too narrow and a
decision going superseded fails to stale its dependents. That trade is the
operator's to make, and it cannot be reviewed sensibly as an argument in prose
-- it needs the typed option surface the rest of this repository's integrations
are reviewed through.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE
TITLE: Fail safes for fingerprints that reference a state main cannot reconstruct
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
A fingerprint is a content hash of a parent's contract fields, not a Git
object, so squashing does not invalidate it. What squashing destroys is the
ability to RECONSTRUCT the accepted state: if a parent hashed to X only on an
intermediate commit that the squash erased, a later suspect report can say the
edge is stale but not what changed.

Two checks:

- Every recorded hash corresponds to a parent state reachable in main.
- No recorded hash is ahead of the branch's common base with main; if one is,
  rebase before accepting, so acceptances are taken against states that will
  survive the squash.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Without these, an acceptance taken mid-branch against a parent that changed
again before merge leaves a dangling reference the moment the branch squashes.
The mechanism still reports the edge as suspect, which is correct, but it loses
the diff that makes the report actionable -- and a report that only says
"something changed" is the one people learn to ignore.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Merge-time discipline may subsume both. If acceptance is taken as the last step
before merge, the recorded hash is by construction the state that becomes main.
Worth checking whether the two checks are then redundant or still needed as a
backstop against acceptances taken earlier in the branch.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-CHECK
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-PREMERGE-GC
TITLE: Keep fingerprint lineage during development, collapse it before merge
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Permit fingerprints referencing states between the branch head and its common
base with main while the branch is in development, then run a specialized
garbage collection before merge that erases the ones the squash is about to
make unreachable.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Full lineage across a multi-commit pull request is genuinely useful while
working -- it is the trace of how a design was reasoned through. It becomes
dangling references the instant the branch squashes. Collecting at the boundary
keeps the value and pays the cost once.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Needs grooming, and possibly a plan revision rather than a mechanism: it may
change how MECH-FP-CHECK reports rather than being a separate step. Not settled
that a garbage collection is the right shape.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-ACCEPT-READINESS
TITLE: Refuse to sign a fingerprint against a parent that is still moving
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
fp-accept refuses to record a hash for a parent that is not yet stable:
a MECHANISM, SLICE, INVARIANT or SPIKE below interface-settled, or a DECISION
whose STATUS is open.

No new field is needed. DEPTH already distinguishes still-moving from stable,
and STATUS does the same for decisions.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Accepting a contract asserts that no further change is expected. A hash taken
against a node someone is still editing is meaningless: it goes stale the next
day, and the resulting suspect report trains the reader to dismiss suspects.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-CHECK
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS
TITLE: Git operations that change node content before it reaches main
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Squash is SAFE. It preserves the final tree, so a content hash is byte
identical before and after and matches the branch tip. Only the intermediate
commits are lost, which costs diagnostics rather than correctness.

Rebase is the hazard, in two ways:

- Rebasing onto a main that changed a node you depend on makes the parent's
  content genuinely different, so a recorded hash goes stale. This is correct
  behavior arriving at the wrong moment: during a mechanical operation, where
  the reader is not thinking about design and learns to dismiss the report.
- Conflict resolution can produce node content that neither side authored. The
  result is a contract nobody accepted, created silently by a merge.

Restacking is a series of rebases and therefore the second hazard repeatedly.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
None of this bites until a branch reaches main; before that it is local churn
and a re-accept costs nothing. Merging to main is the forcing function, so the
handling is deliberately deferred until the first merge -- by which point the
approach may have changed enough that a solution designed now would be wasted.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
What is NOT yet designed: whether a rebase should re-accept automatically when
the parent's change is confined to fields outside the fingerprint, and how to
detect content produced by conflict resolution rather than by an author. Both
want a real merge to look at rather than speculation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-CHECK
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-FIELD-TUNING
TITLE: Tune the contract field selection once the option surface exists
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Revisit which fields a fingerprint covers, through the Nix option surface,
after the prototype has been in use. The prototype's hardcoded selection is a
starting point chosen to exercise the logic, not a settled answer.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The option surface is not load bearing for building the detector, so waiting on
it would block work for no gain. But the selection does need the operator's
review eventually, and reviewing it as prose was already rejected.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE
  ROLE: Governed_By
